{
  "id": "scl-024",
  "pattern": "scope-closures",
  "difficulty_rating": 1600,
  "explanation": "`var i` is function-scoped, not block-scoped, so there is only ever one `i` shared by every closure pushed into `counters`. By the time any of the returned functions actually runs, the loop has already finished and `i` has settled at its final value, `n` (3). All three closures read that same variable, so readAll(3) returns [3, 3, 3] instead of the intended [0, 1, 2].",
  "prompt": "makeCounters is meant to hand back one closure per index, each remembering its own `i`. Step through readAll(3) and predict what it returns.",
  "language": "javascript",
  "snippet": "function makeCounters(n) {\n  const counters = [];\n  for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    counters.push(function () {\n      return i;\n    });\n  }\n  return counters;\n}\nfunction readAll(n) {\n  const fns = makeCounters(n);\n  return fns.map(fn => fn());\n}\nconsole.log(readAll(3));",
  "interaction": "scrubber",
